WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.217 --> 00:00:11.928
TSMC has made a major breakthrough in next-gen memory

00:00:11.928 --> 00:00:14.305
Does it mean Samsung is losing the throne to TSMC?

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:15.724
Even in the memory sector?

00:00:15.765 --> 00:00:17.017
In early 2024

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:18.685
ITRI and TSMC announced

00:00:18.727 --> 00:00:21.688
they will co-release the world’s fastest MRAM

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:23.314
SOT-MRAM

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:25.859
It could take the lead in the market of

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.360
Next-Gen AI memory

00:00:27.402 --> 00:00:29.779
The new-type memory not only accesses data extremely fast

00:00:29.779 --> 00:00:30.947
with low power consumption

00:00:30.947 --> 00:00:33.533
It will surely be more and more crucial in the future

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:35.118
with larger and larger servers

00:00:35.118 --> 00:00:36.995
What’s great with this memory is

00:00:37.037 --> 00:00:38.288
It’s not bothered by blackouts

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:39.914
It doesn’t lose its data

00:00:39.956 --> 00:00:41.624
All great things in one

00:00:41.708 --> 00:00:43.251
How did they make it?

00:00:43.251 --> 00:00:44.335
Besides MRAM

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:46.046
What other new-type memories

00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:47.714
will be the ace of manufacturers

00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:49.799
in the memory market?

00:00:53.344 --> 00:00:54.679
We mentioned in the last episode

00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:56.931
to swiftly load and save data

00:00:56.973 --> 00:00:58.433
it’s better to use capacitors

00:00:58.433 --> 00:01:00.477
rather than heavy magnets

00:01:00.518 --> 00:01:02.353
A transistor plus a capacitor

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:04.522
is the simplest memory unit

00:01:04.522 --> 00:01:06.191
With or without electricity in the capacitor

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:08.818
means 1 or 0 as a digital bit

00:01:08.818 --> 00:01:10.195
When you need to load data

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:11.696
or rewrite it

00:01:11.696 --> 00:01:13.656
Just turn on the switch of transistors

00:01:13.656 --> 00:01:16.242
to load or write with capacitors

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:17.869
But in reality, it’s not that easy

00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:19.746
Even if the transistor is off

00:01:19.788 --> 00:01:22.582
It still leaks electricity slowly

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:23.750
So the design needs to

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:26.127
recharge the capacitor constantly

00:01:26.169 --> 00:01:27.337
to refresh memory

00:01:27.378 --> 00:01:29.714
We can’t just call it leaking memory

00:01:29.714 --> 00:01:32.383
so it’s called “dynamic” random access memory

00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:34.427
written as Dynamic RAM

00:01:34.469 --> 00:01:35.720
or the acronym DRAM

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:36.930
And if there’s

00:01:36.971 --> 00:01:38.056
a long blackout

00:01:38.098 --> 00:01:40.558
the electricity in the capacitors will drain out

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.060
All the temporarily saved data

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.144
will disappear

00:01:43.144 --> 00:01:44.187
So, this memory

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:46.481
is also called volatile memory

00:01:46.481 --> 00:01:48.650
If the data needs to be preserved for a long time

00:01:48.691 --> 00:01:50.026
You can’t put it here

00:01:50.026 --> 00:01:52.403
It has to be saved in hard disc drive or USB flash drive

00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:54.781
In “non-volatile” flash memory

00:01:54.823 --> 00:01:56.866
volatile and non-volatile memories

00:01:56.908 --> 00:01:57.867
each has its own demerits

00:01:57.909 --> 00:02:00.161
For example, DRAM of volatile memories

00:02:00.203 --> 00:02:01.538
needs to be constantly recharged

00:02:01.538 --> 00:02:04.791
In reality, the frequency is about once every 64 milliseconds

00:02:04.833 --> 00:02:06.709
15.6 times per second

00:02:06.751 --> 00:02:08.586
When a computer has to add memory

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:09.963
especially now with AI models

00:02:10.004 --> 00:02:10.964
AI PCs

00:02:11.005 --> 00:02:11.548
AI phones

00:02:11.548 --> 00:02:12.966
that evolve endlessly

00:02:13.007 --> 00:02:14.134
These computers

00:02:14.175 --> 00:02:16.678
will grow to be power-devouring monsters

00:02:16.719 --> 00:02:18.263
And non-volatile memories

00:02:18.263 --> 00:02:19.514
have their problems too

00:02:19.514 --> 00:02:21.057
Though flash memory among them

00:02:21.057 --> 00:02:22.851
doesn’t lose data during a blackout

00:02:22.892 --> 00:02:26.062
Yet in the process of frequent writing and erasing data

00:02:26.104 --> 00:02:29.149
High voltage will gradually damage the oxide layers in structure

00:02:29.149 --> 00:02:31.484
So the write-in times of a flash memory

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:32.652
is limited

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:34.237
Short life is the demerit

00:02:34.279 --> 00:02:36.072
Besides, the greatest defect is

00:02:36.114 --> 00:02:38.533
loading and writing speed much slower than DRAM

00:02:38.533 --> 00:02:39.409
namely

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.119
in the next-gen memory market

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:42.829
the goal is really clear

00:02:42.871 --> 00:02:44.747
Can we develop a memory

00:02:44.747 --> 00:02:46.749
with combined merits

00:02:46.791 --> 00:02:47.625
which is fast

00:02:47.625 --> 00:02:48.418
long-life

00:02:48.418 --> 00:02:49.502
energy-saving

00:02:49.544 --> 00:02:50.712
and not bothered by blackouts

00:02:54.299 --> 00:02:56.384
To develop a new-type memory

00:02:56.384 --> 00:02:59.012
Scientists began to research with different materials and structures

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:01.514
One of them is magnetoresistive memory

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:02.390
MRAM

00:03:02.432 --> 00:03:03.308
It means literally

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:05.685
to store data with magnetic force

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:06.519
Wait a minute

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:07.854
Didn’t we say in the last episode that

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:09.439
magnetism is an option

00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:11.232
eliminated in the 1970s?

00:03:11.274 --> 00:03:12.358
Well, it is

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:15.069
But previous magnets were big and heavy

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:16.362
The magnets used this time

00:03:16.404 --> 00:03:18.948
are much smaller than those in the 1970s

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:20.867
We must look further into the origin

00:03:20.909 --> 00:03:22.535
How on earth did the magnets

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:24.412
produce their magnetism?

00:03:24.454 --> 00:03:26.539
The principle behind it is quite complicated

00:03:26.581 --> 00:03:27.332
Let’s just take the

00:03:27.332 --> 00:03:28.958
most simplified version

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:29.751
Try to imagine

00:03:29.834 --> 00:03:30.585
a magnet consists of many

00:03:30.627 --> 00:03:33.922
tiny weeny magnets

00:03:33.922 --> 00:03:36.341
The little magnets are electrons in the material

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:38.718
Why are electrons magnetic?

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:39.677
In quantum dynamics

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:42.847
you can’t visually observe every electron rotating

00:03:42.847 --> 00:03:45.725
but there’s a quantum feature called spin

00:03:45.725 --> 00:03:47.852
This spin generates magnetism

00:03:47.894 --> 00:03:51.022
and the magnetic force points to a specific direction

00:03:51.022 --> 00:03:53.316
like a small compass

00:03:53.358 --> 00:03:55.777
OK, to understand how MRAM works

00:03:55.818 --> 00:03:57.362
this is all we need to know

00:03:57.403 --> 00:04:00.323
magnetism is related to electron spin

00:04:00.323 --> 00:04:01.699
And the direction of that spin

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:03.701
is related to the magnetized direction

00:04:03.785 --> 00:04:04.661
Here comes the point

00:04:04.702 --> 00:04:05.870
When an electron tries to

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:08.039
pass through some magnetized material

00:04:08.039 --> 00:04:09.332
if its spin is consistent

00:04:09.374 --> 00:04:11.334
with the material’s magnetized direction

00:04:11.376 --> 00:04:14.045
It can easily pass through

00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:15.713
like merging into its own kind

00:04:15.713 --> 00:04:16.339
Contrarily

00:04:16.381 --> 00:04:17.966
if an electron tries to pass through a bunch of

00:04:18.007 --> 00:04:20.260
peers with opposite spin direction

00:04:20.301 --> 00:04:21.678
It will be seriously obstructed

00:04:21.719 --> 00:04:24.180
namely confronting larger resistance

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:26.349
It’s called the giant magnetoresistance effect

00:04:26.349 --> 00:04:28.142
Though there are records of humans

00:04:28.142 --> 00:04:30.019
using magnets before Christ

00:04:30.061 --> 00:04:31.646
the giant magnetoresistance

00:04:31.646 --> 00:04:33.648
wasn’t discovered until 1988

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:36.651
and it won the Nobel Prize in physics in 2007

00:04:36.734 --> 00:04:39.362
How can we use the giant magnetoresistance

00:04:39.404 --> 00:04:41.072
to design memories?

00:04:41.072 --> 00:04:43.449
Just like a capacitor stores 1 and 0

00:04:43.491 --> 00:04:45.868
by the status of with or without electricity

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:47.078
giant magnetoresistance

00:04:47.078 --> 00:04:49.956
can represent 1 and 0

00:04:49.998 --> 00:04:51.624
by the status of low and high resistance

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:52.500
In MRAM

00:04:52.542 --> 00:04:54.085
There are two magnetic materials

00:04:54.085 --> 00:04:56.212
clamping a non-magnetic material

00:04:56.212 --> 00:04:57.005
The purpose is

00:04:57.046 --> 00:04:58.214
to control the magnetized direction of

00:04:58.214 --> 00:05:00.550
each magnetized material

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:02.719
the spin direction of an electron is not fixed in a circuit

00:05:02.719 --> 00:05:04.595
There are up and down

00:05:04.637 --> 00:05:05.805
Yet when two materials

00:05:05.847 --> 00:05:07.932
both got magnetized direction upwards

00:05:07.932 --> 00:05:09.183
a spin upward electron

00:05:09.225 --> 00:05:10.727
can easily pass through the first

00:05:10.768 --> 00:05:11.644
and the second

00:05:11.686 --> 00:05:13.062
the whole resistance is smaller

00:05:13.104 --> 00:05:15.148
And if the magnetized directions of two materials

00:05:15.189 --> 00:05:15.857
are opposite

00:05:15.857 --> 00:05:17.233
Whether it’s upward

00:05:17.233 --> 00:05:18.026
or downward

00:05:18.067 --> 00:05:20.653
It’s hard for an electron to pass through both materials

00:05:20.695 --> 00:05:23.448
This is when the whole resistance value gets larger

00:05:23.448 --> 00:05:25.950
In real memory frameworks

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:28.828
It’s usually made as a double ferromagnetic layer

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:30.830
The magnetized direction of one layer is fixed

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:32.123
to the right, for example

00:05:32.165 --> 00:05:34.250
then write the bit in the second layer

00:05:34.292 --> 00:05:37.045
make the magnetized direction to the right if you want to record 1

00:05:37.086 --> 00:05:38.046
The magnetized direction to the left

00:05:38.046 --> 00:05:39.672
If it’s a 0

00:05:39.714 --> 00:05:41.966
The direction will be kept

00:05:42.008 --> 00:05:43.176
When you load data later

00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:45.011
If the current goes from up to down

00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:46.220
measure the value of resistance

00:05:46.262 --> 00:05:48.556
You know the magnetized directions of the two layers

00:05:48.598 --> 00:05:50.058
are consistent or opposite

00:05:50.099 --> 00:05:52.977
get the 1 or 0 result by it

00:05:52.977 --> 00:05:55.813
That’s how magnetoresistive memory works

00:05:55.855 --> 00:05:57.231
The merit of magnetoresistive memory is

00:05:57.231 --> 00:05:59.025
magnetism can be stored for a long time

00:05:59.067 --> 00:06:01.444
hence a non-volatile memory

00:06:01.486 --> 00:06:02.362
Compared to the magnetic core memory used in the 1960s

00:06:02.403 --> 00:06:04.947
that we mentioned in the last episode

00:06:04.947 --> 00:06:06.157
The unit is small

00:06:06.157 --> 00:06:08.034
and convenient to access

00:06:08.076 --> 00:06:09.494
almost like a DRAM

00:06:09.494 --> 00:06:11.788
Surely MRAM has its bottleneck in development

00:06:11.788 --> 00:06:13.623
that’s how to control precisely

00:06:13.623 --> 00:06:15.750
the magnetized direction of a thin ferromagnetic layer

00:06:15.792 --> 00:06:17.835
without affecting other bits

00:06:17.877 --> 00:06:20.671
The earliest way is to put a wire

00:06:20.713 --> 00:06:21.881
around the ferromagnetic layer

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:24.384
use the electromagnetic field generated by the current in the wire

00:06:24.384 --> 00:06:26.177
to change the polarized direction

00:06:26.219 --> 00:06:28.054
But to prevent the wire-generated magnetic field

00:06:28.096 --> 00:06:29.764
from affecting the bits around

00:06:29.806 --> 00:06:31.766
every memory unit has to keep a certain distance

00:06:31.808 --> 00:06:33.351
from each other

00:06:33.351 --> 00:06:35.728
It’s a huge limitation for downsizing

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:38.314
so it’s hard for MRAM to be competitive

00:06:38.314 --> 00:06:40.358
until around the year 2000 AD

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:42.068
Scientists made a major discovery

00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:43.778
making MRAM to come back

00:06:44.362 --> 00:06:45.446
If we let a current pass through

00:06:45.488 --> 00:06:47.824
some upward-polarized magnet layer first

00:06:47.824 --> 00:06:49.283
to screen the electrons

00:06:49.325 --> 00:06:52.328
most of the rest are upward rotating electrons

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:53.538
then let the current pass through

00:06:53.538 --> 00:06:56.874
a second unpolarized ferromagnetic layer

00:06:56.916 --> 00:06:58.960
It would apply a moment of force

00:06:58.960 --> 00:06:59.919
to the electrons in the second layer of material

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:02.713
forcing them to turn to the same rotating direction

00:07:03.339 --> 00:07:05.425
It’s like a group of dancers with specific steps

00:07:05.466 --> 00:07:06.259
entering the pool

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.678
to change the step direction of the entire pool

00:07:08.678 --> 00:07:09.929
Rotating polarized electrons

00:07:09.971 --> 00:07:12.348
also change the magnetized direction of the magnetic material

00:07:12.348 --> 00:07:13.808
without an external magnetic field

00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:15.017
The phenomenon is called

00:07:15.017 --> 00:07:16.352
“Spin-transfer torque”

00:07:16.394 --> 00:07:18.146
In short, STT

00:07:18.187 --> 00:07:20.648
Compared to the way we just mentioned , using the external wire-generated

00:07:20.648 --> 00:07:22.817
magnetic field to change the magnetized direction

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:24.861
STT doesn’t need an external magnetic field

00:07:24.902 --> 00:07:26.863
simply change the ferromagnetic layer’s polarized direction

00:07:26.904 --> 00:07:29.073
by a current

00:07:29.115 --> 00:07:31.075
to write the bit

00:07:31.117 --> 00:07:33.244
without affecting nearby bits

00:07:33.286 --> 00:07:34.245
So it solved the problem of

00:07:34.287 --> 00:07:36.664
bits cannot get too close

00:07:36.664 --> 00:07:38.624
The product using STT technology

00:07:38.666 --> 00:07:40.918
is called STT-MRAM

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:43.212
It fulfills the demand for modern computing size

00:07:43.212 --> 00:07:44.213
hence in 2006

00:07:44.255 --> 00:07:46.466
became the first batch of commercially mass-produced MRAM

00:07:50.261 --> 00:07:51.846
To understand the latest progress in this realm

00:07:51.888 --> 00:07:53.431
we must ask the experts, of course

00:07:53.431 --> 00:07:54.765
Today we’re honored to have a special guest

00:07:54.807 --> 00:07:57.059
professor Hu Bi-he of the Department of Electrical Engineering, NTU

00:07:57.101 --> 00:08:00.313
to share with us the latest progress in next-gen memory

00:08:00.313 --> 00:08:02.523
Professor Hu, we’d like to know

00:08:02.565 --> 00:08:04.734
we saw the ITRI &amp; TSMC

00:08:04.734 --> 00:08:06.652
released in 2024

00:08:06.694 --> 00:08:09.155
a new product called SOT-MRAM

00:08:09.197 --> 00:08:10.865
What’s the difference with

00:08:10.865 --> 00:08:13.534
what we mentioned earlier as STT-MRAM

00:08:13.576 --> 00:08:14.911
that was popular several years ago?

00:08:14.952 --> 00:08:18.039
The so-called SOT means

00:08:18.039 --> 00:08:19.207
Spin Orbit Torque

00:08:19.248 --> 00:08:22.543
Its write-in operation mode is different from STT

00:08:22.585 --> 00:08:24.545
using the spin Hall effect

00:08:24.587 --> 00:08:26.547
The write-in mode of SOT-MRAM

00:08:26.589 --> 00:08:28.841
isn’t using the spinning polarized current

00:08:28.883 --> 00:08:30.384
through the magnetic material layers

00:08:30.384 --> 00:08:31.761
but to let the current

00:08:31.761 --> 00:08:33.221
flow by them

00:08:33.221 --> 00:08:35.973
Separating the current routes of

00:08:36.015 --> 00:08:36.974
write-in and loading

00:08:36.974 --> 00:08:39.810
make the memory significantly superior

00:08:39.852 --> 00:08:42.021
in terms of operation speed

00:08:42.021 --> 00:08:44.941
also reduce the power consumption

00:08:44.982 --> 00:08:47.777
Besides the SOT-MRAM released by TSMC

00:08:47.777 --> 00:08:50.363
we’ve actually heard of many different types of memories

00:08:50.404 --> 00:08:52.156
Can you elaborate on them for us

00:08:52.198 --> 00:08:54.075
what’s different about them?

00:08:54.075 --> 00:08:55.826
Besides SOT-MRAM

00:08:55.826 --> 00:08:57.078
There are actually at least three other types

00:08:57.119 --> 00:09:00.081
of mainstream emerging memory technologies

00:09:00.122 --> 00:09:02.583
Firstly, I’ll introduce a type called resistive random access memory

00:09:02.625 --> 00:09:03.292
RRAM in short

00:09:03.334 --> 00:09:04.502
The RRAM

00:09:04.544 --> 00:09:07.004
has two metal layers

00:09:07.046 --> 00:09:09.298
clamping a layer of transition metal oxide

00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:11.300
to form a sandwich structure

00:09:11.342 --> 00:09:12.969
changes the resistance value of the transition metal oxide

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:15.680
by applying different voltages

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:17.223
to the states of 0 and 1

00:09:17.223 --> 00:09:19.517
This is the first type of resistive memory

00:09:19.559 --> 00:09:21.936
The second type is called

00:09:21.978 --> 00:09:23.062
phase-change memory

00:09:23.104 --> 00:09:25.565
Its structure also looks like a sandwich

00:09:25.565 --> 00:09:26.732
but the middle layer material

00:09:26.732 --> 00:09:28.693
consists of vulcanized chalcogenide

00:09:28.734 --> 00:09:32.029
a glass-like material

00:09:32.071 --> 00:09:33.656
By heating it up

00:09:33.698 --> 00:09:35.950
you can change the chalcogenide layer

00:09:35.992 --> 00:09:37.368
Its crystal phase

00:09:37.368 --> 00:09:39.745
can make it a multi-crystal state

00:09:39.745 --> 00:09:41.872
or a non-crystal status

00:09:41.914 --> 00:09:43.332
getting various resistance values

00:09:43.332 --> 00:09:45.501
by changing the crystal phase

00:09:45.543 --> 00:09:47.920
to make a state of 0 or 1

00:09:47.962 --> 00:09:49.380
The third type of memory

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:50.756
is ferroelectric RAM

00:09:50.798 --> 00:09:53.759
Ferroelectric RAM is actually quite an old memory

00:09:53.759 --> 00:09:56.345
Conventionally, we use Perovskite

00:09:56.345 --> 00:09:57.847
as the ferromagnetic layer material

00:09:57.847 --> 00:09:59.515
But in 2011

00:09:59.557 --> 00:10:01.058
Scientists found a kind of

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:02.643
widely used

00:10:02.643 --> 00:10:04.937
high dielectric coefficient semiconductor material

00:10:04.979 --> 00:10:06.355
called hafnium oxide

00:10:06.397 --> 00:10:09.984
At the technology node of 4 to 5 nanometers

00:10:10.026 --> 00:10:12.028
it can be introduced to minimize

00:10:12.069 --> 00:10:15.072
transistors’ effective thickness of the Gate Oxide

00:10:15.114 --> 00:10:16.782
Scientists found in 2011

00:10:16.782 --> 00:10:19.910
it’s has a ferroelectric feature besides its high dielectric coefficient

00:10:19.952 --> 00:10:22.538
This drew interest because

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:24.540
a widely used material

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:26.125
means it does not pollute

00:10:26.167 --> 00:10:27.335
or disrupt the entire production line

00:10:27.335 --> 00:10:28.419
hence highly acceptable

00:10:28.461 --> 00:10:30.546
There have been many research projects in recent years

00:10:30.546 --> 00:10:32.548
progressing rapidly

00:10:32.548 --> 00:10:34.133
The operation of Ferroelectric RAM

00:10:34.175 --> 00:10:35.635
largely relies on a ferroelectric layer

00:10:35.635 --> 00:10:37.345
and the reversible electric dipole in it

00:10:37.386 --> 00:10:39.680
By reversing the positive and negative charge direction of electric dipole

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:42.642
it allows the current of transistors beneath

00:10:42.683 --> 00:10:44.018
flow through more easily

00:10:44.018 --> 00:10:45.144
or with more resistance

00:10:45.186 --> 00:10:47.730
making a high or low current state

00:10:47.772 --> 00:10:50.650
to form 0 and 1 states

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:53.277
Do you believe these next-gen memories

00:10:53.319 --> 00:10:56.072
will change the entire memory market?

00:10:56.113 --> 00:10:58.866
Can it change the entire memory market

00:10:58.908 --> 00:11:01.869
I think the idea might not exactly

00:11:01.911 --> 00:11:03.454
a concept widely accepted

00:11:03.496 --> 00:11:06.374
What we have to consider is whether these emerging memories

00:11:06.374 --> 00:11:07.875
be used to compensate for

00:11:07.917 --> 00:11:10.920
the gap in the existing memory hierarchy framework

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:12.963
The memory hierarchy framework we have now

00:11:13.005 --> 00:11:15.257
we haven’t mention previously

00:11:15.341 --> 00:11:16.300
we haven’t mentioned previously

00:11:16.342 --> 00:11:18.344
The fastest would be S-RAM

00:11:18.386 --> 00:11:20.012
which is a cache memory

00:11:20.012 --> 00:11:21.639
then comes the main memory, DRAM

00:11:21.681 --> 00:11:23.641
Their operation speeds are both at the nanosecond level

00:11:23.683 --> 00:11:24.934
Very fast

00:11:24.975 --> 00:11:28.729
But SRAM requires the most basic memory unit

00:11:28.729 --> 00:11:30.815
of at least six transistors

00:11:30.815 --> 00:11:32.316
so it’s bulky

00:11:32.316 --> 00:11:34.652
bulky means high cost

00:11:34.694 --> 00:11:37.446
It can’t be of a very large capacity

00:11:37.446 --> 00:11:39.407
like other memories

00:11:39.448 --> 00:11:40.658
That’s right

00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:43.577
Besides SRAM &amp; DRAM with high operating speed

00:11:43.619 --> 00:11:45.830
USB drives or solid state drives

00:11:45.830 --> 00:11:47.665
people use frequently

00:11:47.707 --> 00:11:48.874
use a technology

00:11:48.916 --> 00:11:50.334
we call flash memory

00:11:50.376 --> 00:11:51.669
That’s right

00:11:51.711 --> 00:11:54.171
The merit of flash memory is its low cost

00:11:54.213 --> 00:11:56.340
also its high capacity

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:58.384
People’s hard disc drives typically have at least

00:11:58.426 --> 00:12:00.845
1TB to over 2TB

00:12:00.886 --> 00:12:02.221
but their operation speed is much slower

00:12:02.221 --> 00:12:03.931
at the microsecond level

00:12:03.973 --> 00:12:05.141
So you can imagine

00:12:05.141 --> 00:12:07.017
nanosecond &amp; microsecond levels

00:12:07.059 --> 00:12:08.519
in the system

00:12:08.561 --> 00:12:11.647
with a significant speed gap in between

00:12:11.689 --> 00:12:13.566
The greatest potential of emerging memories is

00:12:13.607 --> 00:12:16.277
their ability to make up for this speed gap

00:12:16.318 --> 00:12:19.447
There’s a wonderful merit in the meantime

00:12:19.447 --> 00:12:21.282
It’s a non-volatile memory

00:12:21.282 --> 00:12:22.533
just like flash memory

00:12:22.575 --> 00:12:25.035
That’s right

00:12:25.035 --> 00:12:26.579
A so-called non-volatile memory means

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:28.372
when you turn off the power

00:12:28.414 --> 00:12:29.665
the data remains

00:12:29.665 --> 00:12:32.585
the homework and photos students saved

00:12:32.585 --> 00:12:35.212
are in this type of non-volatile memory

00:12:35.212 --> 00:12:36.672
Volatile memory

00:12:36.714 --> 00:12:38.174
is what we mentioned of higher speed

00:12:38.215 --> 00:12:39.175
SRAM and DRAM

00:12:39.216 --> 00:12:41.010
Only when we boot the computer

00:12:41.051 --> 00:12:42.011
turn on the power

00:12:42.011 --> 00:12:46.849
can it load data saved somewhere else

00:12:46.849 --> 00:12:49.351
as temporary stored memory

00:12:49.393 --> 00:12:50.978
But they can’t store your homework

00:12:50.978 --> 00:12:52.646
or photos

00:12:52.646 --> 00:12:53.939
That’s a gap

00:12:53.939 --> 00:12:56.650
Emerging memories have got higher speed

00:12:56.734 --> 00:12:58.569
operating near the nanosecond level

00:12:58.611 --> 00:13:00.446
while remaining non-volatile

00:13:00.446 --> 00:13:01.614
Hopefully it can make up for

00:13:01.614 --> 00:13:04.950
the existing memory hierarchy gap

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:07.077
To make the system achive

00:13:07.119 --> 00:13:08.954
better performance at a lower cost

00:13:08.996 --> 00:13:10.956
So I wouldn’t say that emerging memories

00:13:10.998 --> 00:13:13.667
will change the entire memory market

00:13:13.709 --> 00:13:15.753
What we should consider is whether these emerging memories

00:13:15.795 --> 00:13:18.839
make up for what can’t be done now

00:13:18.839 --> 00:13:20.758
make the whole system operate with

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:21.926
better performance

00:13:21.926 --> 00:13:25.179
Emerging memories are still very promising

00:13:25.179 --> 00:13:27.348
even the MRAM we discussed the most in this episode

00:13:27.389 --> 00:13:29.183
People have long expected it to be

00:13:29.225 --> 00:13:30.476
an universal memory

00:13:30.518 --> 00:13:34.772
MRAM itself also operates at the nanosecond level

00:13:34.814 --> 00:13:36.941
It can probably replace

00:13:36.941 --> 00:13:38.984
the slowest rank of cache memory

00:13:38.984 --> 00:13:41.445
which is jokingly called the last cache memory

00:13:41.445 --> 00:13:43.531
with being non-volatile

00:13:43.572 --> 00:13:45.616
You can minimize power consumption

00:13:45.616 --> 00:13:47.701
because you don’t need to reload the data

00:13:47.743 --> 00:13:48.702
to recovery

00:13:48.744 --> 00:13:51.956
It’s a non-volatile feature

00:13:51.997 --> 00:13:54.458
With more read/write cycles

00:13:54.458 --> 00:13:57.253
With more load/write times

00:13:57.294 --> 00:14:00.172
there’s a potential to replace existing cache memory

00:14:00.172 --> 00:14:02.842
that we use as a storage device

00:14:02.883 --> 00:14:04.468
These emerging memories

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:06.262
have great potential depending on

00:14:06.262 --> 00:14:07.555
different applications

00:14:07.596 --> 00:14:09.515
If we try to make a demand list

00:14:09.515 --> 00:14:11.016
for memory

00:14:11.058 --> 00:14:13.644
or predict which type will prevail

00:14:13.644 --> 00:14:16.438
in reality

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:17.481
is like this:

00:14:17.481 --> 00:14:20.276
We can’t simply rely on just one type of memory

00:14:20.276 --> 00:14:22.611
We need to integrate the merits of various memories

00:14:22.653 --> 00:14:25.030
to cope with different applications

00:14:25.072 --> 00:14:27.616
Let’s take an example

00:14:27.616 --> 00:14:28.993
The cellphones we use

00:14:29.034 --> 00:14:31.704
or high power consuming, high-performance servers

00:14:31.745 --> 00:14:34.164
It’s actually a relative thing

00:14:34.206 --> 00:14:36.876
We know the power demand is very large

00:14:36.917 --> 00:14:38.836
then in memory applications

00:14:38.836 --> 00:14:41.046
we might need to develop higher speed memories

00:14:41.046 --> 00:14:44.049
But it may consume more power

00:14:44.091 --> 00:14:45.885
to meet the demand of existing servers now

00:14:45.885 --> 00:14:48.178
we still need high speed memory like that

00:14:48.178 --> 00:14:51.056
But with some common wearable devices

00:14:51.098 --> 00:14:53.434
they might need very low power consumption

00:14:53.434 --> 00:14:55.311
and the operating speed doesn’t have to be so fast

00:14:55.352 --> 00:14:56.812
there’s no need for high speed demand

00:14:56.896 --> 00:14:57.855
So the design of memory

00:14:57.855 --> 00:15:00.024
has to be optimized as

00:15:00.065 --> 00:15:01.525
a low power consuming one

00:15:01.525 --> 00:15:03.485
but more reliable

00:15:04.403 --> 00:15:06.780
without needing to recharge frequently

00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:07.948
This is our demand

00:15:07.948 --> 00:15:09.450
So to cope with different needs

00:15:09.533 --> 00:15:11.410
memories will have different specializations

00:15:11.452 --> 00:15:12.870
Thanks for your elaboration

00:15:12.912 --> 00:15:15.247
With AI and consumer electronics market growing vigorously

00:15:15.289 --> 00:15:16.457
Whether it’s the semiconductor market of

00:15:16.457 --> 00:15:18.250
logic chips or memory

00:15:18.250 --> 00:15:20.085
competition will only become more intense

00:15:20.085 --> 00:15:21.629
Whoever holds the new design

00:15:21.629 --> 00:15:23.047
will gain an early advantage

00:15:23.088 --> 00:15:24.673
and dominate the entire market

00:15:24.715 --> 00:15:26.133
In this competition

00:15:26.133 --> 00:15:28.010
there’s another realm that can’t be ignored

00:15:28.052 --> 00:15:30.679
besides chip architecture tech

00:15:30.721 --> 00:15:32.806
That’s the equipment for manufacturing chips

00:15:32.848 --> 00:15:33.307
For example

00:15:33.307 --> 00:15:34.975
when China is unable to obtain

00:15:35.017 --> 00:15:37.227
ASML’s EUV machine

00:15:37.227 --> 00:15:40.522
it‘ll impact the development of the whole country’s semiconductor industry

00:15:40.564 --> 00:15:42.733
What is EUV used for?

00:15:42.816 --> 00:15:44.818
What are the key factors in the semiconductor manufacturing process

00:15:44.818 --> 00:15:46.278
we have to pay attention to?

00:15:46.278 --> 00:15:47.655
The answers to these questions

00:15:47.655 --> 00:15:49.323
shall be uncovered in our next episode

00:15:49.323 --> 00:15:51.033
See you next time

